    for k, v in mu.items(): a(f"* mu95 {k}: asymptotic {v['mu95_asymptotic']:.2f}; toys (straight-line fit of ln CLs vs mu over {len(v['points'])} points, {v['points'][0]['n_sb']} sb + {v['points'][0]['n_b']} b toys per point) {v['mu95_toy_fit']:.2f} +- {v['mu95_toy_fit_err']:.2f}" + (f"; interpolation {v['mu95_toy_interp']:.2f}" if v.get('mu95_toy_interp') else "") + ".")
    a("\nmu95 toy comparison (VH mu95 toys: R4 used 4 mu points x 240 toys (previously 5 x 480); the change 6.41 -> 6.82 mixes the R4 workspace correction with the grid/toy-statistics change. Toy mu95 minus asymptotic, in units of the toy fit error; the fit error is UNDERSTATED because the same seeds are reused at every mu point and the CLs curve is non-monotone): " + "; ".join(f"{k}: asymptotic {v['mu95_asymptotic']:.2f} vs toy {v['mu95_toy_fit']:.2f} +- {v['mu95_toy_fit_err']:.2f} ({(v['mu95_toy_fit']-v['mu95_asymptotic'])/v['mu95_toy_fit_err']:+.1f} sigma)" for k, v in mu.items() if v.get('mu95_toy_fit')) + ". The toys constrain mu95 only at this level (a +-10-25 % toy-fit error band at 400-480 toys per point, understated by the seed reuse) and mu95 is NOT called validated or confirmed by them. Earlier toy realisations on the previous template normalisation gave WH 6.85 and 8.34 with the same fit (work/toys_preR4). The asymptotic mu95 uses a refined scan grid (v1.2 fix).")
    for k, v in lm.items(): a(f"* lambda=-1 {k}: asymptotic Z {v['Z_asimov']:.2f}; toy median-q Z {v['Z_toy_median']:.2f} (q 16-84 %: Z {v['Z_toy_16']:.2f}-{v['Z_toy_84']:.2f}); {v['n_distinct_q']} distinct q values in {v['ntoys']} toys; {100*v['frac_q_lt_1']:.0f} % of the toys have q < 1" + ("; PLATEAU: the median bracket is degenerate, its error is not meaningful" if v['plateau'] else "") + ("; NOT COMPARABLE with the Asimov value (the q distribution is far from asymptotic)" if k.endswith("ZH") else "") + f"; convention: {CONV[k.split(chr(58))[0]]}.")
    a("\nlambda toys: q(lambda=-1) of SM toys is a DISCRETE variable at these counts (few events in 4 mT bins (WH) or 30 bins with 2-3 events (ZH)); the toy Z is sqrt of the 50 % quantile of q over the toys (a plateau value when many toys share the same q). The base of q is the minimum of the profile over the SM point and a lambda grid {-2, -1.5, ..., 3} (vh_coup.py --toys), not the exact global minimum: slightly conservative. For ZH alone the toy median q sits on such a plateau (kZ-sign: median q " + "%.2f with %.0f %% of the ZH toys at q < 1, against the Asimov q = %.3f; kW-sign: %.2f with %.0f %%, Asimov %.3f" % (t["lambda_m1"]["kZ-sign:ZH"]["toy_median_q"], 100*t["lambda_m1"]["kZ-sign:ZH"]["frac_q_lt_1"], t["lambda_m1"]["kZ-sign:ZH"]["asimov_q"], t["lambda_m1"]["kW-sign:ZH"]["toy_median_q"], 100*t["lambda_m1"]["kW-sign:ZH"]["frac_q_lt_1"], t["lambda_m1"]["kW-sign:ZH"]["asimov_q"]) + "): for q << 1 the median is fluctuation-dominated and is not comparable with the Asimov value; both mean no ZH lambda sensitivity. WH and VH agree with Asimov within 4-6 % in Z. Seeds: the toys of this version are seeded per chunk (np.random.seed(seed); the lambda toys use default_rng(seed)); the first toy production was not seed-reproducible and is archived (work/toys_prefix/).")
